<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Divashtich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 722</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Divashtich was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a> in Sogdiana who in the early 720s claimed the titles king of Sogdiana and lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. Resisting the Umayyad Arab conquest, he retreated to the castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh">Mount Mugh</a>, where he was captured in 722 and crucified. His archive of documents in Sogdian, found at Mount Mugh in 1932–1933, is a key source on Sogdian society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/divashtich.jpg" alt="Divashtich" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ALFGRN · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Possible_Sogdian_King_Devashtish_or_God_Verethranga,_Aini_district,_Tajikistan,_7-8th_century_CE,_National_Museum_of_Antiquities_of_Tajikistan_(A_1060).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Divashtich, also spelled Devashtich, Dewashtich and Divasti, was the son of Yodkhsetak, of a noble Sogdian dehqan family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> that traced its descent to the Sasanian king Bahram V Gur. The family bore the title of sur and had ruled parts of Sogdia since the 6th century; Divashtich was the last of its five members to bear that title. Like most of his subjects he followed a Sogdian form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, shaped by other religions, in which gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nanaya</a> were worshipped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In about 706 he was elected king of Panjikant, succeeding the Turkic prince Chukin Chur Bilga, and the city reached the height of its prosperity under him. He shared power with other princes. Some of his coins name a certain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a>, either the goddess Nanaya or Chukin Chur Bilga's daughter, whom he may have married. When Tarkhun, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, was overthrown in 709/710 for his pro-Muslim policy and replaced by Gurak, Tarkhun's two sons fled to Divashtich's court, strengthening his own claim to Samarkand.</p><p>After the invasion of the Arab general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a>, Divashtich and other Sogdian rulers acknowledged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in about 712. While Gurak sought help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, Divashtich stayed loyal, so much so that he was even thought a Muslim. In 719 he had to hand Tarkhun's sons to al-Jarrah ibn Abdallah, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and as late as 721 he corresponded with the new governor, Abd al-Rahman ibn Nu'aym al-Ghamidi. In 720, however, he and Karzanj are named as leaders of an anti-Arab rebellion in Sogdia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The rebels' ally at-Tar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Farghana</a> betrayed Karzanj to the Umayyad general Sa'id ibn Amr al-Harashi, who defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> and massacred more than 3,000 Sogdian inhabitants of the city. Al-Harashi then beat Divashtich in battle in 722. Divashtich fled to the fortress of Kal'ai Mug, about 60 kilometres from Panjakent, and surrendered. The Umayyad governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> wanted him freed, but al-Harashi had him crucified on a Zoroastrian burial building and sent his head to Iraq, a choice that later counted in al-Harashi's removal. His son Tarkhun was taken to Iraq as a prisoner; a descendant, Mikal ibn Abd al-Wahid, founded the Mikalid family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Divashtich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its ruler <b>Divashtich</b> styled himself king of Sogdiana and ikhshid of Samarkand.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-panjikent">Principality of Panjikent</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in Tajikistan. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.66,66.98&amp;year=712" aria-label="Samarkand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Divashtich"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5458 4164h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5458 4164h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.66,66.98&amp;year=712">What was here in 712 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
